+/* ---- [vsync] off: the window's pace ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
+ * What holds GT5's pace is the shared runtime's, the same on every renderer: the host loop's 59.94 Hz beat
+ * (boot_main.cpp, cellGcmTickVBlank) and flips completed no sooner than 16.4 ms apart (cellGcmSys.c
+ * cellGcmTickFlip, GCM_FLIP_EARLY): the game makes at most about 61 frames a second. What differs is how often
+ * the window is given a picture. DirectX 12 presents from the host loop, once per flip it completes, and leaves
+ * out a present with nothing drawn since the last (rsx_live_draw_present_needed). This renderer presents at every
+ * flip command the FIFO walker runs (the draw engine's sink_flip) and at every host present call, with no such
+ * check, and before the host has counted a flip the host loop presents each pass (about every 5 ms). With
+ * vertical sync on, FIFO held those to the screen's refresh; with it off nothing did: GT5's first menus were
+ * shown about 200 times a second, the same picture again and again, the GPU busy for nothing.
+ * Now, while presents don't wait for the refresh, the window gets a picture no sooner than 16.4 ms after the
+ * last one, the flip rule's own floor: a present sooner than that is left out (nothing the game made is lost:
+ * it can't make frames faster), and the latest frame, if one was left out, is shown by the next present or by
+ * the window's pump once the 16.4 ms are up (vk_free_owed). The game itself is not slowed: nothing waits.
+ * VK_FREE_PACE=0: as before (every present shown); VK_FREE_PACE=<us>: another floor (tests). */
+static struct { u64 last; int owed; u32 us; int init; } s_free;
